{
  // The recommended stack for a project using this workflow: the kit's
  // TypeScript adapter plus the four gates this workflow leans on —
  // acceptance (the Gherkin feature file), coverage, build, and e2e.
  //
  // Set a project up:
  //
  //   gates --init                            # writes the config files declared below
  //   cp <workflow>/templates/gates.json .    # then drop in this fuller stack
  //
  // The `arch` and `mutation` gates read `.dependency-cruiser.cjs` and
  // `stryker.conf.cjs`; `stryker-vitest-runner.mjs` is the plugin that fixes
  // Stryker's broken vitest runner and makes `coverageAnalysis: "perTest"` work
  // (10x the wall clock on a real project, same verdicts). All three live in the
  // workflow's `templates/`, vendored from the kit and tracked by
  // `tools/vendored.sha256`, so `gates --init` can write them into a project with
  // no other checkout on the machine.
  //
  // `gates --list` shows the stack and warns about any config it cannot find.
  "files": [
    { "from": "dependency-cruiser.cjs", "to": ".dependency-cruiser.cjs" },
    { "from": "stryker.conf.cjs", "to": "stryker.conf.cjs" },
    { "from": "stryker-vitest-runner.mjs", "to": "stryker-vitest-runner.mjs" }
  ],
  //
  // Order is cheapest-first, so a failure costs the least time. Two ordering
  // constraints are load-bearing: `unit` emits the coverage file `crap` reads,
  // and `coverage` re-emits it for the CRAP check on the full suite. The `e2e`
  // gate builds and serves the real artifact, so it goes last.
  //
  // Two gates carry output assertions. Both are there because the tool
  // otherwise exits 0 having done nothing, and a green gate that checked
  // nothing is worse than no gate. Read their `why` before editing them.
  "note": "The recommended stack. Delete what you do not have.",
  "gates": [
    {
      "name": "types",
      "run": "npx tsc -b",
      "why": "the code type-checks under strict mode",
      "timeout": 300
    },
    {
      "name": "lint",
      "run": "npx oxlint src tests",
      "why": "the project's lint rules hold",
      "timeout": 300
    },
    {
      "name": "depth",
      "run": "depth --baseline .depth-baseline.json",
      "changed_run": "depth --changed --baseline .depth-baseline.json",
      "timeout": 300,
      "advisory": true,
      "why": "no NEW shallow method, pass-through method, leaked literal or shape, co-change without a dependency, needless export, forwarded parameter, comment that repeats its line, or declaration comment narrating the implementation — the nine of Ousterhout's fourteen red flags that are decidable from the source text. Ratcheted against .depth-baseline.json, so the gate means 'never worse than the baseline'; create it once with `depth --update-baseline`. A missing baseline is an error, not a pass. For the design the flags cannot decide, `depth report` prints six readings — shallow modules, deletion candidates, single-implementation abstractions, pure blocks, drift, trend — and never fails a build. Advisory until you have read the baseline you accepted and triaged it once; a heuristic on prose earns the right to fail then, not before."
    },
    {
      "name": "unit",
      "run": "npx vitest run tests/unit",
      "why": "every named behavior of the core holds",
      "timeout": 600
    },
    {
      "name": "acceptance",
      "run": "npx vitest run tests/acceptance",
      "why": "the scenarios in features/*.feature hold. The feature file is parsed and executed, not transcribed — a scenario that cannot run is a scenario that is not a spec.",
      "timeout": 600
    },
    {
      "name": "coverage",
      "run": "npx vitest run --coverage --coverage.thresholds.lines=80 --coverage.thresholds.statements=80 --coverage.thresholds.functions=80 --coverage.thresholds.branches=70",
      "why": "the core is exercised, and this emits the coverage file the crap gate reads. Must run before crap. Coverage is the weakest gate in this stack — it counts the lines that ran, not the ones that were checked, and a suite can reach 100% line coverage with a mutation score under 20%. Worth having as a floor, not worth trusting as a verdict; mutation outranks it. Lower the thresholds for a young project, and raise them as the suite grows.",
      "timeout": 900
    },
    {
      "name": "crap",
      "run": "crap src/core --coverage coverage/coverage-final.json --threshold 10",
      "changed_run": "crap src/core --changed --coverage coverage/coverage-final.json --threshold 10",
      "why": "no function is both complex and under-tested. CRAP is complexity squared times the cube of the uncovered fraction, so it separates a complex function that is tested from one that is not — which a maintainability index cannot, because MI mostly measures size.",
      "timeout": 300
    },
    {
      "name": "arch",
      "run": "npx depcruise -c .dependency-cruiser.cjs src",
      "expect": "[1-9][0-9]* modules",
      "why": "the layers only point inward. The `expect` is load-bearing: dependency-cruiser cannot load TypeScript 7, so it cruises zero modules, prints 'no dependency violations found' and exits 0. Without the assertion that silent pass is a green gate that checked nothing.",
      "timeout": 300
    },
    {
      "name": "dry",
      "run": "npx jscpd --min-lines 8 --min-tokens 60 --format typescript --ignore '**/*.test.ts' src",
      "why": "no duplicated block above the threshold",
      "timeout": 300
    },
    {
      "name": "build",
      "run": "npx vite build",
      "why": "the artifact actually builds",
      "timeout": 600
    },
    {
      "name": "e2e",
      "run": "npx playwright test",
      "why": "the shipped artifact does the job in a real browser",
      "timeout": 900
    },
    {
      "name": "mutation",
      "run": "mutate-changed --full",
      "changed_run": "mutate-changed",
      "reject": "Ran (0\\.00|NaN) tests per mutant",
      "why": "the assertions detect changed behavior. `--full` is the deliberate audit — every mutant, the whole suite, no cache — so run it when you mean it. `gates --changed` scopes the run to what changed and to the tests that cover it, which is the difference between minutes and seconds: measured on a fixture with one unrelated 1.6s suite, 15.5s whole tree against 3.4s scoped, the same result either way. Two ways this gate lies, both with a signature the `reject` names. One: Stryker's vitest integration reports every mutant as survived — verified again on Stryker 10 with vitest 5, 12 of 12 survivors on a fixture whose killing assertions are in the test file. The runner selects the right test and sends the right request, then builds its name filter by joining the suite chain with a space, while vitest 5 matches the `>`-joined full name: nothing matches, zero tests execute, and a mutant nothing runs against cannot be killed. Reproduce it with `npx vitest run <file> -t \"a b\"` (skips) against `-t \"a > b\"` (runs); it is unfixed in the latest runner, and `stryker-vitest-runner.mjs` in this stack's files is the fix for it — `stryker.conf.cjs` as shipped already sets `plugins`, `testRunner: "vitest-fixed"` and `coverageAnalysis: "perTest"` for per-test filtering and 3m39s -> 22-40s on a real project, identical verdicts, so all a project needs is `npm i -D @stryker-mutator/vitest-runner`. Two: a command runner left at Stryker's default concurrency times every mutant out and prints a 100% score made of nothing. Read templates/stryker.conf.cjs before you trust a score of 100.",
      "timeout": 3600
    }
  ]
}
